#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ge — work-graph coordinator. Walks a node spec, calls one worker per node, keeps a ledger.

    ge plan   <spec.json>                 topological order and node kinds
    ge run    <spec.json> [--executor claude|codex|cursor|grok|stub] [--run-id X] [--model M]
                          [--input k=v ...] [--max-cost USD] [--no-baseline] [--resume RUN --from guard|verify]
    ge note   <run-dir> "<text>"          record one human intervention
    ge report <run-dir> [--out FILE]      ledger -> run metrics (no artifact bodies)
    ge ledger [--spec S] [--role R] [--since ISO] [--json]   pass rate / rework / tokens per role (spec/node)
    ge lesson <spec.json> <node> "<text>" append a one-line lesson for a producing node
    ge new    <name> --cwd DIR --target FILE [--workflow patch|research]   scaffold a spec
    ge workflows                          list scaffold layouts
    ge fabric [query] [--show N|--check|--sync]   look for a prompt skeleton (Fabric patterns, MIT)
    ge contract-sha <spec.json> [--write] compute contract.inputs sha256
    ge approve <run-dir> <item>           approve an out-of-scope item blocked by the acceptance gate
    ge trace  <run-dir> [--node N] [--attempt K]   dump a worker's raw output to a file
    ge progress [--limit N] [--json]      node-by-node status of recent runs
    ge stats --topologies [dirs...]       common node/edge shapes; which match a recipe, which to promote
    ge check-model                        check structural invariants (formal/*.als) with Alloy

Principles:
  - The coordinator is code. Slotting, dedup checks and totals happen here. A worker sees one node.
  - Worker output is forced to a JSON schema. Schema failure = rework (retry with the errors attached).
  - The verify node is a separate call (ideally a different model). Only verify reads the rubric.
  - The runner does not judge. It records facts in the ledger. Acceptance is the verify node's verdict.
  - Artifact bodies never go into metrics. Paths, hashes and counts only.

Spec (JSON):
  {"name": "...", "nodes": [
     {"id": "draft", "kind": "model", "prompt": "x/draft.md", "inputs": [], "schema": {...},
      "fanout": "<node with items[]>"?, "retry": 2},
     {"id": "verify", "kind": "verify", "prompt": "x/verify.md", "rubric": "x/rubric.md", "inputs": ["draft"]},
     {"id": "apply", "kind": "script", "script": "x/apply.py", "args": [], "inputs": ["verify"]}
  ]}
  In a prompt file, {{input.<id>}} is that node's artifact (JSON), {{rubric}} the rubric text,
  {{item}} / {{item.<key>}} the fanout item, {{<k>}} a --input value.
  Relative paths resolve against the spec's directory (or the parent of a `graphs/` directory).
  Node keys: "model", "executor", "cwd", "timeout", "retry", "tools": "read" (grok), "lessons",
  "when": {"node": "route", "key": "branch", "equals"|"in": ...}, "stub_output": "<fixture.json>".
  Optional top-level "contract" adds deterministic hooks (see ge_lib/guard.py).

Ledger: ./.ge/runs/<run-id>/ (or $GE_RUNS_DIR). Worker CLIs are optional and found on PATH or via
GE_CLAUDE / GE_CODEX / GE_CURSOR / GE_GROK. The stub executor needs nothing.
"""
